'''ballot<sup>1</sup>''' (bal′ọt), ''v.'' [Early mod. E. also ''ballat'', ''ballet''; < It. ''ballottare'' = F. ''ballotter'', earlier ''balotter'', = Sp. ''balotar'', vote by ballot; from the noun.] '''I.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To decide upon a question, proposition, or candidacy by casting ballots; take a ballot or a vote by ballot: often with ''for'' in the sense of ‘in relation to’: as, to ''ballot for'' members of a club. See the noun.
{{smaller block|The judges . . . would never take their balls to ''ballot'' against him.
{{right|''North'', tr. of Plutarch, p. 927.}}
The convention did not ''ballot'' until its third day.
{{right|''G. S. Merriam'', S. Bowles, II. 185.}}}}
'''2.''' To bound, as in the bore of a cannon: as, spherical projectiles ''ballot'' in the bore of the piece.—'''3.''' To select by lot; draw lots (for): as, to ''ballot'' for places.

'''II.'''† ''trans.'' '''1.''' To vote for or against by ballot; choose or elect by ballot.
{{smaller block|None of the competitors arriving at a sufficient number of balls, they fell to ''ballot'' some others.
{{right|''Sir H. Wotton'', Reliquiæ, p. 262.}}}}
'''2.''' To choose by lot; select by drawing lots for.

