'''balm''' (bäm), ''v. t.'' [< ME. ''baumen'', ''bamen'', < ''baume'', balm. Cf. OF. ''embaumer'', embalm: see ''embalm''.] '''1.''' To embalm.
{{smaller block|Shrouded in cloth of state!<br>''Balm’d'' and entreasur’d with full bags of spices!
{{right|''Shak.'', Pericles, iii. 2.}}}}
'''2.''' To anoint as with balm or with anything fragrant or medicinal.
{{smaller block|''Balm'' his foul head in warm distilled waters.
{{right|''Shak.'', T. of the S., Ind., i.}}}}
'''3.''' To soothe; mitigate; assuage; heal.
{{smaller block|Oppressed nature sleeps:—<br>This rest might yet have ''balm’d'' thy broken senses.
{{right|''Shak.'', Lear, iii. 6.}}}}
[Obsolete or archaic in all uses.]

'''balmaiden''' (bâl′mā″dn), ''n.'' [< ''bal'' + ''maiden''.] A girl or young woman employed in the mines of Cornwall, England.
{{smaller block|The smock-frock is a survival of a ploughman’s dress, and the Cornish miner and mine-girl (or ''balmaiden'') have a sort of peasant dress.
{{right|''N. and Q.'', 6th ser., IX. 508.}}}}

'''balm-cricket''' (bäm′krik″et), ''n.'' [Earlier ''baum-cricket'', appar. a half translation of G. ''baum-grille'', tree-cricket, < ''baum'', a tree (= E. ''beam''), + ''grille'', a cricket: see ''Gryllus''.] The field-cricket, ''Gryllus campestris''.
{{smaller block|The ''balm-cricket'' carols clear<br>In the green that folds thy grave.
{{right|''Tennyson'', A Dirge.}}}}

